# Build context for ouroboros-ui/Dockerfile.
#
# Named for the Dockerfile rather than the directory because that image builds from the
# **repository root** (it is a Yarn workspace and the lockfile lives there). BuildKit
# reads <dockerfile>.dockerignore ahead of <context>/.dockerignore, so this file governs
# that build and no other — a root .dockerignore would silently apply to every image in
# the repo, and an ouroboros-ui/.dockerignore would apply to nothing at all.
#
# It is an allow-list. With the whole repository as the context, a deny-list quietly
# grows a hole every time a directory is added at the root, and what leaks is not only
# size: docs/, .git/ and four sibling modules have no business in a UI image. Starting
# from `*` means a new path is excluded until someone says otherwise.

# Nothing, unless named below.
*

# The workspace root: what Yarn resolves, installs and pins from.
!package.json
!yarn.lock
!.yarnrc.yml

# The sibling manifests. Yarn resolves every workspace before installing any of them, so
# an absent manifest is a failed --immutable install, not a smaller context. They are
# the manifests only — no sibling module's source enters this image.
# ouroboros-rest joins them when it is scaffolded (#27).
!ouroboros-db/package.json
!ouroboros-engine/package.json

# This module.
!ouroboros-ui

# ...minus what is generated rather than written. Both would be copied over by the build
# and neither is portable between a laptop and the image: node_modules is resolved for
# the host platform, and .next is the output of a previous build.
ouroboros-ui/node_modules
ouroboros-ui/.next
ouroboros-ui/out
ouroboros-ui/coverage
ouroboros-ui/*.tsbuildinfo

# ...and minus prose and the container definition itself, none of which any stage reads.
# Excluding them is not about size — it is that editing a sentence in the README should
# not invalidate the build cache and rebuild the application. `__tests__` is deliberately
# *not* here: `next build` type-checks everything tsconfig includes, and dropping the
# suites would leave the image type-checking less than `yarn build` does on a laptop.
ouroboros-ui/README.md
ouroboros-ui/AGENTS.md
ouroboros-ui/CLAUDE.md
ouroboros-ui/Dockerfile
ouroboros-ui/Dockerfile.dockerignore

# ...and minus anything holding a real value. Nothing in the build reads an env file —
# OURO_REST_URL is supplied to the container at run time — so the only way one could
# reach a layer is by accident. `**/` because the exclusion has to hold for every
# directory the lines above let in, not just the root.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
